WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.630
Our school kitchen has a problem, and

2
00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:03.930
I’ve asked our class to help.

3
00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:06.860
Every lunch must give you enough energy,

4
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:10.520
protein and iron, but the money is tight.

5
00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.230
Can maths find the cheapest plate?

6
00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.930
Today: choices, limits and a

7
00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:18.430
goal, written as a model.

8
00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:20.610
Start with the choices.

9
00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:22.590
How many cups of rice?

10
00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:24.390
Call it r.

11
00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:26.850
How many handfuls of groundnuts?

12
00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:28.350
Call it n.

13
00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:31.490
Each letter is a variable: a

14
00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:33.670
number you decide, with a unit.

15
00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:35.920
Now the limits.

16
00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:39.170
A cup of rice gives two hundred and

17
00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.730
sixty kilocalories, a handful of

18
00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:43.590
groundnuts one hundred and seventy.

19
00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:45.950
Together they must give at

20
00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:47.190
least seven hundred.

21
00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.600
In maths: two hundred and sixty r

22
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.660
plus one hundred and seventy n is

23
00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:55.020
at least seven hundred.

24
00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.790
At least means greater than or equal to.

25
00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:04.790
At most means less than or equal to.

26
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.960
So iron at least five milligrams, and

27
00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.400
groundnuts at most two handfuls.

28
00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.540
Then the goal: the cost.

29
00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:19.490
Rice is fifteen coins a cup,

30
00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:21.670
groundnuts eight a handful.

31
00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:25.470
Fifteen r plus eight n, as

32
00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:26.450
small as possible.

33
00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:30.110
Variables, limits and a goal

34
00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:32.150
together make a model.

35
00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.390
Mathematicians call the limits

36
00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:37.270
constraints, and the goal the objective.

37
00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:39.020
Quick check!

38
00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.040
Two cups of rice and no groundnuts.

39
00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:43.920
Does that meet the energy

40
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.040
limit of seven hundred?

41
00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:48.510
No: two times two hundred and

42
00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:50.630
sixty is five hundred and twenty,

43
00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:52.170
less than seven hundred.

44
00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:54.990
To check any plan, put its

45
00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:56.850
numbers into every limit.

46
00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:58.780
A plan that keeps them all

47
00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:00.140
is called feasible.

48
00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.650
The usual plate, two cups of rice,

49
00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.410
an egg and a cup of milk, has

50
00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:08.050
plenty of energy and protein.

51
00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:10.310
But it has only one point

52
00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:11.670
eight milligrams of iron.

53
00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.120
It isn’t feasible, and it

54
00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:15.320
costs sixty coins.

55
00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.900
The cheapest feasible plate costs

56
00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.240
forty-one coins: rice, groundnuts

57
00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:23.340
and a little greens.

58
00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.960
But what about a classmate who’s

59
00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:27.040
allergic to groundnuts?

60
00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:30.380
A model only knows the rules you give it,

61
00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.680
so add the ones that keep everyone safe.

62
00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:35.970
In the app, build your own plate

63
00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:37.950
and watch each need fill up.

64
00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:40.470
Then press Find the cheapest.

65
00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.100
Which needs are met exactly?

66
00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:45.320
Your turn.

67
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.840
The rule says each pupil gets at

68
00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:52.080
most two handfuls of groundnuts.

69
00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:54.900
Which sign do you write?

70
00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:00.240
Less than or equal to: n is at most two.

71
00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:04.040
Remember: a variable is a choice with a

72
00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.580
unit, a limit is an inequality, and the

73
00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.840
goal is what you make small or big.

74
00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.830
To check a plan, put its numbers in.

75
00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:16.670
Try it yourself: write a model for a

76
00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:19.090
choice of your own, like snacks for a

77
00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:20.470
school trip with your pocket money.

78
00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:23.030
Name the variables, write the

79
00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:24.950
limits with signs, and the goal.

80
00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:27.270
Then check two plans by

81
00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:28.210
putting in the numbers.
